# Selectors — read helpers stay centralized

## Context

`app/src/selectors.ts` (`visibleTasks`, `remainingCount`, `taskCount`,
`currentFilter`) is the sanctioned way to derive data from `AppState` — see
`app/src/index.ts` for the pattern.

## Rule

- New derived/read logic over `AppState` (counts, filters, lookups) is added
  as a new named export in `app/src/selectors.ts`, not inlined at the call
  site.
- Consumers of state read through selectors (`visibleTasks`,
  `remainingCount`, or a newly added selector) rather than reading
  `state.tasks`/`state.filter` directly and re-deriving the same logic.
- Selectors stay pure functions of `(state, ...args) => value` — no
  dispatching, no side effects, no mutation of the input state, and no
  reaching into `store.getState()` themselves (the caller passes `state` in).

## How to verify

1. New read/derive logic over `AppState` appears as an exported function in
   `app/src/selectors.ts` with a matching test in a colocated
   `selectors.test.ts`.
2. `grep -rnE "\bstate\.(tasks|filter)\b" app/src` — catches any field/method
   access off `state.tasks`/`state.filter` (`.filter`, `.map`, `.find`,
   `.length`, or bare access), not just `.filter`/`.map`; matches should only
   appear inside `selectors.ts` and `reducer.ts`, not scattered across other
   consumer files (e.g. `index.ts`). Doesn't catch access via a destructured
   alias (`const { tasks } = state`) — review those by eye.
3. `grep -n "dispatch\|getState()" app/src/selectors.ts` returns no matches —
   selectors never call the store themselves.
4. `cd app && npm run typecheck` passes (selectors keep the same `AppState`
   import shape as the rest of the app).